Scenario 5: Deadlock Prevention
Question: You are implementing a multithreaded system where multiple threads are competing for shared resources. You are concerned about the possibility of a deadlock occurring if two threads try to lock resources in different orders. How would you prevent deadlocks in your system?
Answer: To prevent deadlocks, you can follow a strategy of locking the resources in a defined order. Additionally, you can use a timeout mechanism to avoid deadlocks by not allowing threads to wait indefinitely.
**Explanation of the Code**
**Find the rightmost ascent**
Traverse from right to left to find the first digit that is smaller than its next digit (digits[i] < digits[i + 1]).
Find the smallest digit larger than the ascent:
Traverse from right to left to find the smallest digit that is larger than the digit at index i.
**Swap the digits**
Swap the digits at indices i and j.
Reverse the digits to the right of the ascent:
Reverse the sequence of digits to the right of index i to get the smallest possible number that is greater than the given number.
This algorithm ensures that the next greater permutation of the digits is found, and if no such permutation exists (i.e., the digits are in descending order), the function will return -1. However, in this implementation, we assume there is always a valid next greater number as per the problem statement examples provided.

```java
class Resource {
    private final String name;
    public Resource(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    public synchronized void lock(Resource other) {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " locked " + name);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(50); // Simulate some work
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        other.use();
    }
    public synchronized void use() {
        System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " using " + name);
    }
}
public class DeadlockPrevention {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final Resource resourceA = new Resource("Resource A");
        final Resource resourceB = new Resource("Resource B");
        // Thread 1 locks resources in one order
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> {
                synchronized (resourceA) {
                    System.out.println("Thread 1 locked Resource A");
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(100);
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    }
                    synchronized (resourceB) {
                        System.out.println("Thread 1 locked Resource B");
                    }
                }
        });
        // Thread 2 locks resources in the same order (prevents deadlock)
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> {
                synchronized (resourceA) {
                    System.out.println("Thread 2 locked Resource A");
                    try {
                        Thread.sleep(100);
                    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    }
                    synchronized (resourceB) {
                        System.out.println("Thread 2 locked Resource B");
                    }
                }
        });
        t1.start();
        t2.start();

    }
}
