Sort the sequence in non-decreasing order.
Calculate the product of the last three elements of the sorted sequence, as these will be the largest numbers in the sequence.
Return the product.
Here's the Java implementation of this approach:
**Explanation**
The maxProductOf3Subsequence function takes an array of non-negative integers nums as input.
It sorts the array nums in non-decreasing order using Arrays.sort(nums).
It calculates the product of the last three elements of the sorted array, which will give us the maximum product of a subsequence of length 3 with elements in increasing order.
Finally, it returns the calculated product.
**Example**
For the input array {1, 3, 2, 5, 6, 4}, the function returns 120, which is the maximum product of a subsequence of length 3 with elements in increasing order (3 * 5 * 6 = 90).

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class MaxProductOf3Subsequence {

    public static int maxProductOf3Subsequence(int[] nums) {
        // Sort the sequence in non-decreasing order
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int n = nums.length;

        // Calculate the product of the last three elements
        return nums[n - 1] * nums[n - 2] * nums[n - 3];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {1, 3, 2, 5, 6, 4};
        System.out.println("Max product of subsequence of length 3: " + maxProductOf3Subsequence(nums));
    }
}
