Scenario 1: Dependency Injection
**Q1**
You are working on a project where you need to change the implementation of a service without changing the code that depends on it. How would you achieve this in Spring?
**A1**
In Spring, you can achieve this using Dependency Injection (DI). You can define an interface for the service and have multiple implementations of this interface. In your configuration class, you can specify which implementation should be injected.
**For example**
By changing the implementation in AppConfig, you can switch between different implementations without modifying the client code.

```java
public interface MyService {
    void performService();
}

public class MyServiceImpl1 implements MyService {
    public void performService() {
        System.out.println("Service Implementation 1");
    }
}

public class MyServiceImpl2 implements MyService {
    public void performService() {
        System.out.println("Service Implementation 2");
    }
}

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public MyService myService() {
        // Return the desired implementation
        return new MyServiceImpl1(); // or new MyServiceImpl2();
    }
}

public class Client {

    @Autowired
    private MyService myService;

    public void doSomething() {
        myService.performService();
    }
}
```

Scenario 2: Transaction Management
**Q2**
You have a method in a service class that needs to perform multiple database operations atomically. If one operation fails, the entire transaction should be rolled back. How would you implement this in Spring?
**A2**
In Spring, you can manage transactions declaratively using the @Transactional annotation. Apply this annotation to the method that needs transactional behavior.
**For example**
With @Transactional, if any exception occurs during operation1() or operation2(), the transaction will be rolled back automatically.

```java
@Service
public class MyService {

    @Autowired
    private MyRepository myRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void performOperations() {
        myRepository.operation1();
        myRepository.operation2();
        // If any of the above operations fail, the transaction will be rolled back
    }
}
```

