Design patterns are typical solutions to common problems in software design. They represent…
Design patterns are typical solutions to common problems in software design. They represent best practices used by experienced object-oriented software developers. Design patterns can be divided into three categories:
- Creational Patterns
- Structural Patterns
- Behavioral Patterns
- Here, I'll provide examples of the implementation of some commonly used design patterns in Java.
1. Singleton Pattern (Creational)
The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it.
2. Factory Pattern (Creational)
The Factory pattern provides a way to create objects without specifying the exact class of object that will be created.
3. Observer Pattern (Behavioral)
The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically.
4. Strategy Pattern (Behavioral)
The Strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable. This pattern lets the algorithm vary independently from clients that use it.
5. Decorator Pattern (Structural)
The Decorator pattern allows behavior to be added to an individual object, dynamically, without affecting the behavior of other objects from the same class.
Example Usage
Here’s how you might use these patterns in a main method:
**Explanation**
- Singleton Pattern: Demonstrates the creation of a single instance of a class.
- Factory Pattern: Demonstrates creating different product objects without specifying the exact class.
- Observer Pattern: Demonstrates notifying multiple observers when the state of the subject changes.
- Strategy Pattern: Demonstrates changing the behavior (strategy) of an operation at runtime.
- Decorator Pattern: Demonstrates adding responsibilities to objects dynamically.

```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {
        // private constructor to prevent instantiation
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
